ess is never a need. (3) Propose at the start of the task only when the evidence is in the repository's files or the user asked. Otherwise wait for the first blocked request. (4) On a need, call this tool right before you propose, so the proposal matches the environment's current settings; if it fails, propose nothing and say what remains blocked. Then, unless the user asked for a specific change, decide where it belongs, by this session's environment. If `is_default` is true, all the user's repositories use it, so what only one repository needs stays off it: move this session to an environment in `saved_for_session_repositories` with switch_environment and call this tool again (if none is `switchable_by_you`, propose nothing and tell the user which is saved for this repository and why this session cannot move to it), or, if the list is empty, propose a personal one with create_environment and `switch_session`. Edit the Default when every project would want the change, `session_has_repository` is false, or the list is empty and `switch_session` is not offered. A repo's setup script is never for the Default. In any other environment, edit when `editable_by_you` is true, whichever repository it was made for; otherwise propose a personal environment with create_environment, or tell the user the `not_editable_reason`. request_credential saves on the environment this session runs in. (5) Propose once and say why, with the evidence. If the user declines, do not propose a change for that need again in this session.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"environment_id": {"description": "The environment ID to look up (starts with 'env_'), e.g. from list_environments. Omit to look up the environment this session is running in.", "type": "string"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__get_environment"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Fetch a single transcript event from a Claude Code Remote session by session_id and event_uuid. Returns the event's role, content, isSynthetic, and inbound_origin. Same authorization as list_events.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__get_event", "schema": {"description": "Fetch a single transcript event from a Claude Code Remote session by session_id and event_uuid. Returns the event's role, content, isSynthetic, and inbound_origin. Same authorization as list_events.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"event_uuid": {"description": "The event uuid to read.", "type": "string"}, "session_id": {"description": "The session ID that owns the event.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["session_id", "event_uuid"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__get_event"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Get details for a specific Claude Code Remote session by ID. Returns the session's title, status, status_bucket (working / blocked / review_ready / completed / failed — 'failed' means its last turn errored), creation time, and context. Every returned session carries three model fields: configured_model is the model stored at creation, echoed as stored (it may be an alias or carry a context-window suffix, so normalize before comparing); session_context.model is the model the session is currently set to run (the creation-time model, or a later switch or refusal fallback); external_metadata.last_served_model is the model the CLI ran the latest turn on, which also reflects turn-scoped fallbacks (overload or unavailable) that do not change session_context.model. To detect a switch or fallback in a child session, compare configured_model against both session_context.model and external_metadata.last_served_model; the fallback notices in list_events give the reason. Omit session_id to describe this session.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__get_session", "schema": {"description": "Get details for a specific Claude Code Remote session by ID. Returns the session's title, status, status_bucket 