 to use with request_computer_folder, and the folder this session is attached to, if any. The user normally chooses these folders on each computer, in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control. `folders` holds the ones this session can use: each has an environment_id, the computer's name, the folder's name, and whether the computer is online. An environment_id is an internal id that means nothing to the user: never show one to them or ask them to choose between ids; name the computer and the folder instead. `cannot_be_used` holds folders that are in the user's list but cannot be used from a cloud session, each with a reason and an explanation; they have no environment_id and cannot be requested. A folder can be used with or without Git, but only if it is not the home directory, is not inside a hidden directory directly under it (a name that starts with a dot) or ~/Library, and the Claude app or Claude Code on its computer is a version that can serve a folder from that kind of computer (on Windows, not every version can). If the user asks for a folder under `cannot_be_used`, tell them its explanation, and for reason `not_available` also say that using a folder from a cloud session is not available to everyone yet; never tell them it is missing from their list. A folder in neither list cannot be requested. To get it listed, suggest only the Claude desktop app on that computer (Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control), and say that page may not offer a way to add folders. Once added, and once that computer is serving it, it should appear here in one of the two lists, but it may still not be usable: using a folder from a cloud session is not available to everyone yet. Say that plainly, and that they can still do the work in a session that runs on that computer. Do not guess the reason or put it down to their plan or organization. Do not tell the user to run `claude remote-control` to add a folder: for most accounts that command offers nothing to a cloud session, even while it is running, and a folder it does offer is already listed here. Call this before request_computer_folder, when the task needs files, tools or credentials that only exist on the user's computer. Once per session is normally enough: call it again only if the user says they changed the list, or a request was refused because its names did not match. It only reads; it connects nothing. A folder whose `online` is false cannot answer: do not request it. Tell the user that computer is not connected right now (Claude is not running there, or Remote Control is off), ask them to open it, then call this again.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_computer_folders", "schema": {"description": "List the folders on the user's own computers that THIS session can ask to use with request_computer_folder, and the folder this session is attached to, if any. The user normally chooses these folders on each computer, in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control. `folders` holds the ones this session can use: each has an environment_id, the computer's name, the folder's name, and whether the computer is online. An environment_id is an internal id that means nothing to the user: never show one to them or ask them to choose between ids; name the computer and the folder instead. `cannot_be_used` holds folders that are in the user's list but cannot be used from a cloud session, each with a reason and an explanation; they have no environment_id and cannot be requested. A folder can be used with or without Git, but only if it is not the home directory, is not inside a hidden directory directly under it (a name that starts with a dot) or ~/Library, and the Claude app or Claude Code on its computer is a version that can serve a folder from that kind of computer (on Windows, not every version can). If the user asks for a folder under `cannot_be_used`, tell them its explanation, and for reason `not_available` also say that using a folder from a cloud se